	"nodes":[
		{
			"id":"a24e428836fc45de",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## POI's\n\n### The Freshly Carved Star-Eyes → _The Empty Star Watches_\n\n- **Lore Tie:** These new carvings are not the work of mortals. They are the prison’s response to strain, the Wanderer’s “gaze” pressing inward from beyond. The eyes are how mortals _perceive_ the void peering through reality’s thinning veil.\n- **Clue to Drop:** The constellations match those linked to calamity in giant star-charts. Any scholar-type PC might realize the stars are shifting in ways no celestial body should move — proof that the Wanderer’s prison is unraveling.\n- **Foreshadowing:** Staring into the eyes for too long gives visions not of stars, but of **absence**: skies without suns, oceans without water, bodies without souls. This is what the Wanderer offers Eldhoof — not power, but _erasure_.\n\n### The Dome Above → _The Cage Ceiling_\n\n- **Lore Tie:** The dome is a metaphysical barrier — the very fabric of the Wanderer’s prison, woven into the heavens. What players glimpse as flickering stars are actually fractures in the veil.\n- **Clue to Drop:** The hum or pulse they feel in their chests syncs with these flickers. Each “beat” is the prison groaning against the strain of keeping the Wanderer contained.\n- **Foreshadowing:** A failed Wisdom save while gazing up makes a player feel _small, fragile, temporary_. A successful one gives them clarity: “This dome is not a roof — it is a lid.”\n\n### The Frozen Windows → _The Otherworldly Veins_\n\n- **Lore Tie:** The magical ice isn’t natural. It’s reality’s scar tissue, a seal grown over a wound. Each window reveals not the mountain but alternate planes — reflections of where the Wanderer’s entropy gnaws.\n- **Clue to Drop:** The different landscapes seen through the ice (deserts, drowned worlds, ruined cities) are not random. They are glimpses of places where the Wanderer’s whispers have already seeded corruption. Perhaps one is even tied to Eldhoof’s early experiments.\n- **Foreshadowing:** If a player breaks the ice, the silence shatters with it — and the hum becomes a scream. The room begins to echo with whispers in the players’ own voices, promising them the same “immortality” Eldhoof sought.\n\n### The Floor Spiral → _The Weak Point_\n\n- **Lore Tie:** The spiral’s convergence marks the metaphysical heart of the prison. The giants used this very point to channel their “Great Silence” ritual. The warmth beneath is not heat — it’s entropy bleeding upward.\n- **Clue to Drop:** A detect magic reveals threads of necrotic energy woven into the floor, frayed and failing. This is the tether Eldhoof’s necromancy gnaws at.\n- **Foreshadowing:** Standing at the spiral’s center aligns a player’s heartbeat with the chamber’s. For a moment, they hear a voice not in words but in _intent_: the desire to unmake, to erase them utterly. If they resist, it passes. If not, the voice lingers.\n\n### The Silence Itself → _The Great Silence of the Giants_\n\n- **Lore Tie:** The unnatural silence here is a lingering echo of the giants’ final gambit — their attempt to starve the Wanderer by silencing themselves. But the chamber hums still, proving their efforts were futile.    \n- **Clue to Drop:** Comprehend Languages (or even just high Wisdom) interprets the hum as the word _“Witness.”_ This is exactly how the Wanderer’s whispers often manifest: not commands, but simple imperatives. Witness. Obey. Unmake.\n- **Foreshadowing:** A failed save while focusing on the silence implants a single alien image in the mind: the **Empty Star** falling across the sky. The player knows, instinctively, that this is not a metaphor.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2080,
			"y":-1687,
			"width":960,
			"height":1220
		},
		{
			"id":"0bfff37903382b97",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## The Breaking of the Sanctum (Transition)\n\nThe silence holds. Cold, reverent, eternal. And then —\n\nA _shockwave_ ripples through the stone beneath your feet. Dust and frost fall from the dome above, sprinkling across your shoulders like brittle snow. Another tremor follows, stronger, rattling the pedestals in their places. It is not the slow groan of ice or mountain settling — it is the _impact_ of titanic force meeting titanic force.\n\nAt first, the chamber resists. The silence strains to hold, as though the stone itself is unwilling to admit what lies beyond. But the violence outside is too great. With a thunderous crack, one of the frozen windows splinters. The ice webbing across its surface fractures, groans, and finally collapses inward in a crystalline avalanche. The muffled glow vanishes, replaced by the raw glare of daylight spilling across the chamber.\n\nThe silence is broken.\n\nThrough the breach, the muffled stillness of the mountain gives way to the _roar_ of battle — deep, resonant bellows in a tongue you don’t understand, the grinding crash of stone against stone, and the booming _report_ of impacts strong enough to shatter cliffs. The mountain itself shakes with every blow.\n\nThe walls of the sanctum shudder, shedding centuries of frost. Shattered windows vomit light into the chamber as the snow outside collapses away, revealing the glacier plateau beyond.\n\n## Phase 1: The Breaking of the Sanctum (Transition)\n\n**Beat 1: The Quake**\n\n- A shockwave rattles the sanctum. Frost falls from the dome.\n- Dex Save (DC 13) or fall prone as the floor lurches.\n\n**Beat 2: Shattered Windows**\n\n- Ice windows fracture and burst outward, snow and shards filling the chamber.\n- Con Save (DC 14) to avoid being staggered back or cut by flying shards (1d6 cold/slashing).\n\n**Beat 3: The Pull Outside**\n\n- The roar of giants fills the silence. The sanctum collapses, ejecting the party into the glacier plateau.\n- Each player chooses how they brace themselves (Athletics, Acrobatics, or even creative magic). Their rolls color how they enter the battlefield (wounded, heroic, or dazed).\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":0,
			"y":-1599,
			"width":760,
			"height":1043
		},
		{
			"id":"300c3181aad748c7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Treasure Horde of the Silent Sanctum\n\nThis horde is found beneath one of the cracked pedestals, revealed when players investigate the sanctum after session start. The giants left these not as wealth, but as tools and memories.\n\n### Coin & Gemstones\n- 3,500 gp worth of ancient **giant-minted silver discs**, each the size of a human hand. \n- 1,200 gp in **shard-gems**, violet crystals that glow faintly when near necrotic magic. Risky to carry: prolonged exposure causes mild corruption.\n- 1x **Star Sapphire (750 gp)**, carved with a spiral of eyes — faint warmth pulses when held under starlight.\n\n### Mundane Treasures\n- **Rune-Etched Drinking Horn**: When filled, the liquid never spills. Liquids taste faintly of cold iron.\n- **Obsidian Mirror (3 ft. tall)**: When polished, the reflection lags slightly. Any PC attuned to corruption sees their scar flare brighter here.\n- **Bone-Flute of Vigmar**: Carved from mammoth tusk, once used in rituals of silence. When played, it mutes all sound within 30 ft. for 1 round.\n\n### Magic Items\n\nThe PC's Find a Crafting Horde to the Left.\n\n1. **Amulet of the Seal (Rare, Requires Attunement)**  \n   - Grants resistance to necrotic damage.  \n   - Once per long rest, the bearer can cast *Silence* centered on themselves (15 ft. radius, concentration).  \n   - *Drawback:* While worn, whispers intrude during rests: *“Witness.”*  \n\n2. **Glacier-Tear Pendant (Uncommon)**  \n   - A teardrop of frozen water that never melts.  \n   - Wearer can cast *Absorb Elements (Cold only)* once per short rest.  \n   - If broken, releases a 15 ft. burst of freezing air (2d6 cold, Con save half).  \n\n3. **Fragment of the Seal (Wondrous, Minor Artifact)**  \n   - A jagged shard of crystal, thrumming faintly.  \n   - Acts as a **spellcasting focus**.  \n   - When a spell is cast through it, the caster may add +1d4 damage (any type) but gains 1 Corruption Point.  \n   - *Foreshadowing:* One of the actual pieces tied to Ornn/Vigmar’s eternal watch. Its use weakens the barrier further.  \n\n### Curiosities (Story Hooks)\n- **Stone Tablet of Oaths:** An oathbound relic. If a PC swears upon it, the oath burns into their flesh as a faint rune. Breaking it may trigger consequences later.  \n- **Frozen Feather of a Roc:** Blackened, but faintly thrumming. May serve as a spell component for summoning magic.  \n- **Chalice of the Silent Vigil:** Wine poured into it becomes tasteless but grants resistance to cold damage for 1 hour. Drinking too much risks numbness (DM call).  \n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
